// §9.4.1 / Table 9-1. The display family is split down the middle by the analog
// context column, and the split is by RADIX VARIANT, not by task:
//
//     $display                            Yes  Yes
//     $displayb , $displayh , $displayo   Yes  No
//     $strobe                             Yes  Yes
//     $strobeb , $strobeh , $strobeo      Yes  No
//     $write                              Yes  Yes
//     $writeb , $writeh , $writeo         Yes  No
//     $monitor                            Yes  Yes
//     $monitorb , $monitorh , $monitoro   Yes  No
//     $monitoron , $monitoroff            Yes  No
//     $debug                              No   Yes
//
// The reason is in §9.4.3: the b/h/o suffix only sets a DEFAULT radix for
// arguments given without a format specification, and a default radix is a
// statement about bit vectors. An analog block's expressions are reals and
// integers, and §9.4.3 already gives it the per-argument spelling — 06_display_-
// formats.va prints `%b`, `%o` and `%h` from a perfectly legal $display. So the
// suffixed spellings add nothing the analog context can use, and Table 9-1
// withdraws them rather than defining what they would mean.
//
// One file per table, not one per name: the rule is the column, and the first
// diagnostic ends the compile anyway. $monitoron/$monitoroff are here too —
// they are the same column and the same reason, a control over a $monitor
// mechanism §9.4.1 defines in terms of accepted digital steps.
//
// The positive side of every one of these rows is already covered:
// 02_display_display.va, 01_display_strobe.va, 03_display_write.va,
// 04_display_monitor.va, 05_display_debug.va.
//
// A reject, so there is nothing to run and no `want` to write.
//
//! lrm 9.4.1
//! reject analog context
//! reject E0806
module ch09_display_radix_variants_analog_rejected(p, n);
  inout p, n; electrical p, n;
  analog begin
    $displayb(5);
    $displayh(5);
    $displayo(5);
    $strobeb(5);
    $writeh(5);
    $monitorb(5);
    $monitoron;
    $monitoroff;
    I(p,n) <+ V(p,n);
  end
endmodule
